{"id": "13b54a57-ada9-4275-9227-b62c87be80b9", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "You've chosen to triage this in chat, so I'm starting a deep pass now. I'll check the account for what separates a provider-side failure from one on your side. That means error signatures in your logs and traces, egress and network health, and any recent changes or deployments. I'll report back when it's done.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_1tCy2GT1RYlyr00mxZBOPS", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "openai-failure-triage", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["searching-logs", "exploring-metrics", "exploring-traces", "investigating-infrastructure-changes", "discovering-deployments", "discovering-topology", "verifying-with-docs"], "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Determine whether the ~33% failure rate on the agent's direct OpenAI API calls (NOT Bedrock) that began this morning is caused by the provider (OpenAI) or by the customer's own infrastructure/code/config. Produce a verdict with confidence, evidence for each hypothesis, and what is ruled out.",
  "context": "Customer's agent calls the OpenAI API directly for inference over the public internet. Since this morning about a third of calls fail. Customer says their own application metrics look normal (so don't trust app-level dashboards as proof of health; check what is actually observable). Triage is happening ad hoc in chat, read-only. Only AWS account 123456789012 (agent space region us-east-1) is known; discover which regions/workloads/compute (Lambda, ECS/EKS, EC2, API Gateway, etc.) host the agent by looking at topology, log groups, and associations. Check other regions if the workload lives elsewhere.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 123456789012", "region us-east-1 (and any region where the agent workload is found)", "agent compute, its log groups, NAT gateways / egress path, VPC endpoints, security groups, NACLs, route tables, DNS/Route53 Resolver, secrets/param store holding the OpenAI key (names/metadata only, never read secret values)"],
  "timeframe": "From roughly 2026-11-25T00:00:00Z to now (2026-11-25T16:38:00Z); establish the exact onset time of failures and compare against a baseline window from the previous 24-48h.",
  "criteria": "1) Characterize the failures from logs/traces: HTTP status codes (429 rate limit / quota, 401/403 auth or key/billing, 408/timeouts, 500/502/503/504 from provider, 400 request errors), connection-level errors (DNS failure, TLS handshake, connection reset, ETIMEDOUT, SSL errors, proxy errors), error message bodies, request IDs, retry-after headers, which model/endpoint, and whether failures are uniform or concentrated on specific hosts/pods/AZs/NAT gateways/request sizes/models/time buckets. Provider-side signatures: 5xx/overloaded/capacity errors across all hosts, random ~uniform distribution, errors returned from api.openai.com with provider error bodies. Customer-side signatures: 429 from own quota/tier/billing, 401 key issues, failures isolated to one AZ/NAT/host/pod, connection/DNS/TLS errors, port/SNAT exhaustion (check NAT gateway metrics: ErrorPortAllocation, PacketsDropCount, ConnectionAttemptCount vs ConnectionEstablishedCount, BytesOutToDestination), throttling on ENIs, Lambda/ECS concurrency or timeout limits, payload size/token growth, client library/version change. 2) Look at network/egress health in CloudWatch (NAT gateway, VPC flow logs if available, Route53 resolver, ELB/API Gateway metrics, Lambda errors/duration/throttles, ECS/EKS container restarts and CPU/memory). 3) Find changes in the window: CloudTrail (security group/NACL/route/NAT/VPC endpoint/secret/parameter/IAM/Lambda config changes), Kubernetes events, deployments and recent commits/pipeline runs, dependency or SDK version bumps, config/env var changes (e.g., model name, timeout, retry settings, API key rotation). 4) Note: you cannot reach the OpenAI public status page from this account, so say clearly that provider status/incident could not be verified directly from here and suggest the customer check status.openai.com and their OpenAI dashboard (rate limit/usage/billing) as the confirming step; do not claim provider-side cause unless the evidence in this account supports it. Be explicit about what is evidence vs hypothesis, and what could not be checked (missing log groups, permissions). Return a concise report: verdict (provider / customer / undetermined) with confidence, key evidence with timestamps and counts, ruled-out causes, onset time, and 1-3 concrete next steps."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Triage OpenAI call failures: provider vs. our side"}], "created_at": null}

